Nova — NOTICE
=============

Nova is an original work by Sara Sakuragawa, licensed GPLv3+ (see LICENSE).
It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or derived from any proprietary
software. It implements a publicly documented international radio
facsimile format.

Standards basis
---------------
The decoded signal is specified by:

  * WMO-No. 386 (Manual on the Global Telecommunication System),
    Volume I, Part III, section 5 — "Technical characteristics of
    equipment for meteorological facsimile (analogue) transmissions".
    Verified against the 2009 AND 2023 editions; the facsimile
    provisions cited here are identical in the checked sections.

  * ISO 9876:2015, "Ships and marine technology — Marine facsimile
    receivers for meteorological charts" — used as the reference for
    receiver-side behaviour. Nova is DESIGNED TO SATISFY the
    software-applicable requirements of ISO 9876:2015 section 4.2;
    it has not undergone formal type testing, and no claim of
    certified compliance is made.

Third-party algorithm lineage
-----------------------------
Nova reuses and adapts algorithms from the following free-software
projects, under their respective licences. Reuse to date is idea-level:
every algorithm was re-implemented from the public sources, and no
third-party code, tables, or files are present in this tree. The
combined work is GPLv3+.

  * ACfax    — Andreas Czechanowski (DL4SDC), 1995–1998, GPLv2+.
               Quadrature mix-to-baseband and amplitude-normalized
               delay-line discriminator architecture, re-implemented
               in floating point in core/demod.cpp. ACFax's FIR
               coefficient tables are NOT used; Nova computes its FIR
               at runtime. The retained-raw-stream, non-destructive
               adjustment architecture planned for M4 is also ACFax's
               and will be recorded when it lands.
  * HamFax   — Christof Schmitt (DH1CS), 2001–2011, GPLv2+.
               Feature-shape reference only (IOC scaling; the TX path
               exists there alone in the lineage). Its
               FaxDemodulator.cpp carries ACFax's FIR tables and is the
               middle link of the lineage evidence. No code taken.
  * weatherfax_pi (OpenCPN weather fax plugin) — Sean D'Epagnier,
               GPLv3. Via yahfax, an improved adaptation of HamFax.
               Median-with-spread-rejection treatment, re-implemented
               with Nova's measured constants.
  * KiwiSDR FAX extension — jks-prv/KiwiSDR, file-level GPLv3
               (D'Epagnier decoder adapted). Phasing wedge-fit with
               median filtering and the leaky miss-counter idea,
               re-implemented with re-measured constants. Fractional
               sample-rate tracking was surveyed but NOT taken.
  * JWX      — Paul Lutus, 2011–2013, GPLv2+. Studied as prior art
               (PLL demodulation, Goertzel tone detection; clock-
               corrected line accumulation for the sync fold); no code
               taken.
  * Isobar   — Sara Sakuragawa, 2026, GPLv3+ (this project's author's
               own prior work). Per-line resync approach adapted.
  * fldigi   — David Freese (W1HKJ) et al., GPLv3+. Studied as prior
               art. Rule shapes reused with re-measured constants:
               abandon a phasing run after N consecutive failures,
               frequency coherence across a run, and per-line
               correlation kept per line rather than collapsed to a
               histogram mode. No code taken.

No GPLv2+ code is incorporated; where reuse is of GPLv2+-licensed
algorithms, the re-implementation is GPLv3+. If you believe attribution
is missing or incorrect, please open an issue — provenance accuracy is
a project rule, not a courtesy.

Linked libraries
----------------
Distinct from the lineage above, which is idea-level reuse: these are
libraries Nova links against, added 2026-08-13 with the M4 GUI shell.
Neither contributes any algorithm to the decoder. Both are used
unmodified, as installed by the system's package manager.

  * FLTK     — the Fast Light Toolkit, https://www.fltk.org/.
               LGPL with the FLTK exceptions (see the library's own
               COPYING), which state that subclassing FLTK widgets does
               not create a derivative work and that static linking does
               not oblige an application to relicense. Used for the
               window and widgets in gui/.
  * RtAudio  — Gary P. Scavone, 2001–2023, MIT-style permissive licence.
               Used for audio device enumeration and capture in gui/.

Both are linked by the `nova-gui` target ALONE, behind
`option(NOVA_BUILD_GUI)`. The decoder core, the command-line tools and
the entire test suite link neither and build with the option OFF; this
is enforced by the build rather than by convention — a missing library
skips the GUI target instead of failing the build.
